WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.270
For an IBM Cloud customer it's imperative
to be able to run those workloads in the geographies

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.920
that are important to them.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.290
The Weather Company does 250 Billion forecasts
daily.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.260
We complete 13 Billion API calls.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.960
We do 100 Million page views daily.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.540
There is a lot of overhead and upkeep.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:38.330
Red Hat OpenShift on IBM Cloud is fully managed
native OpenShift environment where we are

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:43.690
providing highly available masters, multizone
clusters, integrating with platform services

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:50.270
such as identity and access management, monitoring
and logging, and key protect.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:56.750
It is the same OpenShift that I'm familiar
with and love so much, but the moment we moved

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:02.070
into the IBM Cloud all I had to worry about
was my worker nodes.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.190
That was nice.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.280
In terms of DevOps, the key benefit of using
a managed Kubernetes Service is that it allows

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.960
our customers to focus on innovation
and delivering software faster for their customers.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:23.360
Using a managed Kubernetes service has resulted
in a 80% improvement in efficiency in my workloads

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.570
and my team's workloads because now we are
focused on what The Weather Company needs

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:33.010
us to focus on instead of all this infrastructure
that provided no direct benefit.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:39.000
Together Red Hat and IBM are dedicated to
improving the DevOps experience of using Kubernetes.

